---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `for i = 1, 5 do io.write(" ", i) end
print()
for i = 10, 1, -3 do io.write(" ", i) end
print()
for i = 0, 1, 0.25 do io.write(" ", i) end
print()`;
const Figure1Out = ` 1 2 3 4 5
 10 7 4 1
 0 0.25 0.5 0.75 1`;

const Figure2 = `local fuel = 10

while fuel > 0 do
  io.write(" ", fuel)
  fuel = fuel - 3
end
print()`;
const Figure2Out = ` 10 7 4 1`;

const Figure3 = `local tries = 0

repeat
  tries = tries + 1
until tries >= 3

print("tries:", tries)`;
const Figure3Out = `tries:\t3`;

const Figure4 = `for i = 1, 10 do
  if i > 4 then break end
  io.write(" ", i)
end
print()`;
const Figure4Out = ` 1 2 3 4`;

const Figure5 = `for i = 1, 6 do
  if i % 2 == 0 then goto continue end
  io.write(" ", i)
  ::continue::
end
print()`;
const Figure5Out = ` 1 3 5`;

const Task1 = `-- main.lua - Skynest launch sequence

for i = 5, 1, -1 do
  print("T-minus", i)
end
print("LIFTOFF")`;
const Task1Out = `T-minus\t5
T-minus\t4
T-minus\t3
T-minus\t2
T-minus\t1
LIFTOFF`;

const Task2 = `-- main.lua - Skynest launch sequence

for i = 5, 1, -1 do
  print("T-minus", i)
end
print("LIFTOFF")

local fuel = 20
local distance = 0

while fuel > 0 do
  fuel = fuel - 6
  distance = distance + 10
end

print("Fuel left:", fuel)
print("Distance:", distance)`;
const Task2Out = `T-minus\t5
T-minus\t4
T-minus\t3
T-minus\t2
T-minus\t1
LIFTOFF
Fuel left:\t-4
Distance:\t40`;

const Task3 = `-- main.lua - Skynest launch sequence

for i = 5, 1, -1 do
  print("T-minus", i)
end
print("LIFTOFF")

local fuel = 20
local distance = 0

while fuel > 0 do
  fuel = fuel - 6
  distance = distance + 10
end

print("Fuel left:", fuel)
print("Distance:", distance)

for checkpoint = 1, 8 do
  if checkpoint % 2 == 0 then goto continue end
  print("Scanning checkpoint", checkpoint)
  ::continue::
end`;

const Checkpoint = `T-minus\t5
T-minus\t4
T-minus\t3
T-minus\t2
T-minus\t1
LIFTOFF
Fuel left:\t-4
Distance:\t40
Scanning checkpoint\t1
Scanning checkpoint\t3
Scanning checkpoint\t5
Scanning checkpoint\t7`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.6: Loops</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Concept -->
        <section id="concept">
          <h2>The Idea</h2>

          <p>
            Lua has three loops, and the numeric{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="for"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is the one you will reach for most. It reads as{" "}
            <i>from, to, by</i> &mdash; and{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">both ends are included</b>.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure1}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <CodeSnippet
              code="for i = 1, 5"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            runs five times, ending <i>on</i> 5. There is no{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="i < 5"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            to get wrong and no{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="i++"
              lang={"js"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            to forget &mdash; Lua has no increment operator at all. The third
            number is the step, it may be negative, and it may be a fraction.
          </p>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="io.write"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is new here. It is like{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="print"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            with no tabs and no line break, which is what you want when building
            one line out of many pieces.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Two things the loop variable will not let you do.</b> It only
            exists inside the loop, so you cannot read{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="i"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            after the{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="end"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />. And assigning to it inside the loop does not change how many
            times the loop runs &mdash; Lua worked the count out before the
            first pass.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">When you do not know the count</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="while"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            repeats for as long as its condition holds, and checks{" "}
            <i>before</i> each pass.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure2Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <CodeSnippet
              code="repeat ... until"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is the same idea upside down. It checks <i>after</i> each pass, so{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">the body always runs at least once</b>, and
            the condition says when to <i>stop</i> rather than when to continue.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure3Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            Both of those conditions are inverted relative to each other. If you
            ever translate a{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="while"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            into a{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="repeat"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, the condition has to flip, and forgetting that gives you a loop
            that runs exactly once or never stops.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Getting out early</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="break"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            leaves the loop immediately, in any of the three kinds.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure4}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure4Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b class="text-sn-theme">There is no{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="continue"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            in Lua.</b> This is a genuine gap, and the accepted workaround is a
            jump to a label at the very bottom of the loop body.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure5}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure5Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <CodeSnippet
              code="goto continue"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            jumps forward to{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="::continue::"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, skipping whatever sits between. The label is not a keyword and{" "}
            <i>continue</i> is not special &mdash; you could call it{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="::skip::"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            &mdash; but everyone writes it this way, so write it this way too.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>The label has to be the last thing in the body.</b> A label with
            code after it means you jumped past some lines and then ran others,
            which is not what you meant. And you can usually avoid the whole
            thing by inverting the test into{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="if i % 2 ~= 0 then"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            &mdash; reach for the jump when the alternative is three levels of
            nesting.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Start a fresh <b class="text-sn-theme">main.lua</b>. You are running
            a launch sequence, burning the fuel it takes, and sweeping a row of
            checkpoints on the way out.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Count down and launch</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Count from <b>5 down to 1</b> with a numeric{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="for"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              and a negative step.
            </li>
            <li>Print LIFTOFF after the loop has finished.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="main.lua" />

          <Code
            code={Task1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Burn fuel until it runs out</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Start with 20 fuel and 0 distance. Each pass burns <b>6</b> fuel
              and adds <b>10</b> distance.
            </li>
            <li>
              Loop while there is fuel left, then print both totals.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Look hard at the fuel figure.</b> It is negative, and that is
              genuinely what the code you were asked for does. Work out why
              before reading on, then write the reason in a comment.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "8-17" }}
          />

          <Code
            code={Task2Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            The condition is checked <i>before</i> the pass, not during it. With
            2 fuel left the loop still qualifies, then burns 6 anyway.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">A loop guard says whether to start a pass,
            never whether the pass can afford to finish.</b>
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Scan the odd checkpoints only</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Loop over checkpoints <b>1 through 8</b>.</li>
            <li>
              Skip the even ones using{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="goto continue"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              and a label.
            </li>
            <li>Print a line for each checkpoint you actually scan.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "19-23" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Fix Task 2 so the fuel never goes negative &mdash; the loop should
              only burn what it can afford.
            </li>
            <li>
              Rewrite Task 3 with no{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="goto"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              at all. Two ways exist: invert the condition, or change the loop's
              step.
            </li>
            <li>
              Convert Task 2's{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="while"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              into a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="repeat ... until"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              that behaves the same. Remember the condition has to flip.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Check Your Work</h2>

          <p>Your finished file should produce this.</p>

          <Code
            code={Checkpoint}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            You now have every control structure Lua has. Everything from here
            is about the one data structure you have not met yet &mdash; and
            once you have it, loops become the thing you do <i>to</i> it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-light"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-5"
          >
            <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Previous
          </a>
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-primary"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-7"
          >
            Next: Functions <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
